findings.body:
The `isCurrentUser` check was changed from `filters.userIds.length === 1 && user.id === filters.userIds[0]` to `filters.userIds.includes(user.id)`. The FORBIDDEN guard is `!areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam && !isCurrentUser`, so any filter list that contains the caller now passes, even when other IDs are outside the caller's scope.

::diff{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.ts" lines="146-156"}

Failure scenario: a team admin sends `userIds: [self, victimUserId]`, where the victim is in another team. `areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam` is false, but `isCurrentUser` is true, so no error is thrown. The first union query then runs `WHERE userId IN (self, victim)`, and the attendee and seat queries match the victim's email, so the victim's bookings are returned.

::callout{type="security"}
This is a data-exposure bypass of the scope check. Restore the original exact-match semantics, or require every ID to be either the caller or in the accessible set.

::suggestion{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.ts" lines="146-154"}
const areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam = filters.userIds.every(
  (userId) => userId === user.id || allAccessibleUserIds.includes(userId)
);

if (!areUserIdsWithinUserOrgOrTeam) {
  throw new TRPCError({ code: "FORBIDDEN", message: "You do not have permissions to fetch bookings for specified userIds" });
}

Several new tests end with `.resolves.not.toThrow()`. That assertion does not check the returned bookings or the query arguments, so it passes even if scoping is wrong. The own-ID test at line 277 is one example. The same pattern appears at lines 299 and 333.

::code{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.test.ts" lines="262-278"}

No test covers `userIds: [self, outsider]`, which is the bypass in the critical finding. No test covers `orgId` being forwarded through the handler to the permission service either, which is why the dropped `orgId` in the repository wrapper went unnoticed. These tests give false confidence.

The two child-team branches filter by `orgId` in different ways. The PBAC child branch at line 309 checks only `org.id` or `child.id`. The fallback child branch at line 357 also checks `child."parentId" = orgId`. Because `child` is already joined as `child.parentId = org.id`, that extra predicate is equivalent to `org.id = orgId` and adds nothing.

::code{file="packages/features/pbac/infrastructure/repositories/PermissionRepository.ts" lines="353-358"}

The behavior is currently correct, but the two branches read differently and the redundant clause makes the scoping rule harder to verify. Pick one predicate and use it in both queries.

The diff touches `permission-check.service.ts` beyond the `orgId` change. It removes trailing whitespace in two places and collapses the `getHighestRole` signature onto one line. None of this is needed for the bookings fix, and it makes the review harder.

::diff{file="packages/features/pbac/services/permission-check.service.ts" lines="296-299"}

Keep formatting-only changes out of functional PRs, or land them separately.

`PermissionCheckService.getTeamIdsWithPermission` catches every error and returns `[]`. The bookings handler now depends on this call for its entire visibility scope. If the query fails, for example from a DB timeout or a bad parameter, the user silently sees only their own bookings with no error.

::diff{file="packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings/get.handler.ts" lines="117-122"}

This predates the PR and matches other callers. Still, the bookings list is the place where a silent failure is most confusing. Consider letting the error propagate from the bookings path, or at least logging it at the handler level.
